Explicit (forward) Euler uses the slope at the start of the step:
\[
y_{i+1} = y_i + h\,f(t_i, y_i).
\]
Implicit Euler (backward Euler) instead uses the slope at the end of the step:
\[
y_{i+1} = y_i + h\,f(t_{i+1}, y_{i+1}),
\]
which requires solving for \(y_{i+1}\) at each step (this page uses a few steps of Newton’s method) — but buys stability that explicit Euler lacks. The default example, \(y' = -5y\), is chosen to make this visible: try dragging \(n\) down to see explicit Euler destabilize while implicit Euler stays smooth.

A standard mammogram has 80% sensitivity and 90% specificity: if you have cancer, it comes back positive 80% of the time; if you don’t, it comes back negative 90% of the time. You test positive. What are the odds you actually have cancer? Drag the sliders below — the dots are a simulated population, red for the fraction with the condition, dark for the fraction without.
// VM is the shared utility library, loaded globally via _includes/head-scripts.html (js/**). This page uses VM.ui.renderTable.
VM = window.VM
config = ({
